Generated by: Andrew Teylu <andrew@tey.lu>
Generated on: 2026-08-24
Generator: Model-driven synthetic Latendresse profile generator, with empirical structure extracted from the bundled canonical biological model.
Application: Synthetic linear-real and floating-point benchmarks modeled on the structure of the existing SMT-LIB QF_LRA Latendresse benchmarks [1], which originate in flux-balance-analysis and pathway-database model-completion applications [2].
Biological inspiration: continuous E. coli flux balance analysis, specifically the ecoliFBAyicesTest model in the existing SMT-LIB Latendresse family.
Target solver: cvc5
Time limit: 300

The model has 100 real-valued flux symbols, including a dedicated objReaction, and 59 exact mass-balance rows spanning 1 to 45 reactions. Row widths and stoichiometric coefficients are scaled empirical samples from the canonical E. coli model. Ordinary reactions are boxed at the selected numeric flux scale; one exchange reaction has a narrow positive interval, while objReaction is linked through the stoichiometric system rather than explicitly boxed.

Objective regime: interior. The dedicated objective uses the E. coli family-native assertion direction `(>= threshold objReaction)` and is set to an interior upper threshold (satisfiable).
Numeric profile: mixed-rows. Flux bounds remain at 1000, while selected complete mass-balance rows are multiplied by 1/100 or 100. This is equivalent over the reals but exposes different rounding and conditioning behavior after floating-point translation.

[1] Timothy King, Clark Barrett, and Bruno Dutertre, Simplex with Sum of Infeasibilities for SMT, FMCAD 2013.
[2] Mario Latendresse, Markus Krummenacker, Miles Trupp, and Peter D. Karp, Construction and completion of flux balance models from pathway databases, Bioinformatics 28(3), 388-396, 2012.
|)
